    <DataNode elementId="AT4G34890-MONOMER" textLabel="Xanthine dehydrogenase 1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q8GUQ8" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Arabidopsis has two xanthine dehydrogenase genes, AtXDH1 (At4G34890) and AtXDH2 (At4G34900) that are tandemly duplicated and highly homologous. Both genes are ubiquitously expressed, with AtXDH1 showing altered transcript level in response to aging, senescence, salt and cold stress, and ABA treatment. Both proteins contain a N-terminal domain for binding of two iron-sulfur clusters of the [2Fe-2S] type, followed by a FAD/NAD binding domain, and a C-terminal Moco binding domain. Heterologous expression and characterization of AtXDH1 indicated it is a strict dehydrogenase, not an oxidase. NAD+ but not molecular oxygen acted as the electron acceptor. The enzyme efficiently catalyzes the oxidation of both hypoxanthine and xanthine.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT4G34890-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT4G34890.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Smart" value="SM01092" />
